{
  "_comment": "End-to-end timelines of the whole bridge (src/app.js and everything it wires together) against a fake panel and a real MQTT broker (Mosquitto 2, a private instance per scenario that knows the user bridge/secret, which the bridge's MQTT_USERNAME/MQTT_PASSWORD name, and holds no retained message at the start). Each row of a group is one scenario: the bridge is started, then 'steps' are played in order. Setup: 'login' holds the bridge env USERNAME/PASSWORD, the session value the panel hands out and the u/p the panel accepts for that session (an independent copy of a crypto.json vector, so this file stays self-contained); panel has requireLogin on and the default six zones Predsien, Lava izba, Prava izba, Spalna, Obyvacka, Chodba, status tables of 32 zeros, useraccess [1, 0]; time is simulated: 'advance_ms' moves the clock of the bridge's own timers (poll 1000 ms, keep-alive 3000 ms, MQTT connect timeout 5000 ms) and then waits for the real network effects. Row keys: name, known_bug?, note?, panel?, broker?, start?, steps?. panel: zones ([enabled, name] pairs), statuszone, useraccess, accept_login (false: every login is rejected), down (true: panel is not reachable at startup), faults (list of {path, kind, times?} active from the start; times omitted = for ever). Fault kinds: http_500 (HTTP 500), reset (connection reset before any response), hang (request never answered), login_page (HTTP 200 with the login page), garbage (HTTP 200 with a page that has no status tables), wrong_title (HTTP 200 with a well-formed page titled something else than 'Paradox IP Module'), page (HTTP 200 with the fault's own 'page': an inline string or {file: path relative to tests/}). broker: accept_credentials (false: the credentials the bridge is configured with are unknown to the broker, which refuses the login: CONNACK 5 'not authorised' on Mosquitto, where MQTT 3.1.1 also allows 4 'bad username or password'), silent (the broker accepts the connection and never answers CONNECT), down (true: nothing listens on the broker port), retained ({topic: payload} the broker already holds, published retained by another client before the bridge starts, replayed on SUBSCRIBE). start: ready (default true: wait until the REST port accepts connections; false: startup is expected to fail or hang) and expect (checked once startup is over). Step actions, applied in this order within a step: panel_status ({statuszone?, useraccess?, alarms?} the panel serves from now on), panel_fault (same shape as a fault), panel_expire_session, panel_down, broker_drop_clients (the broker disconnects the bridge's session; the user stays valid), mqtt_command ('arm' | 'disarm' | {topic, payload}: 'arm' is topic paradox/command/arm with an empty payload; published, QoS 0 and not retained, by another client to the broker, which delivers it to the bridge's subscription), mqtt_message ({topic, payload}: a PUBLISH written into the bridge's connection as if the broker had sent it, even for a topic the bridge did not subscribe to, which a real broker never delivers), signal (SIGHUP | SIGINT | SIGTERM delivered to the process), rest ({method, path} request to the bridge's REST API), advance_ms. Expectations (expect) describe what happened DURING that step, i.e. since the previous check; absent keys mean 'nothing': mqtt_published (messages the bridge published, in order, as another client subscribed to paradox/# sees them on the broker; all are QoS 0, retained as listed: the flag the bridge set; the broker's own log of the bridge's PUBLISH packets must show the same topics, QoS, retain flags and payload lengths, so nothing is published anywhere else either), panel_requests (request lines the panel received, in order; at startup absent means not checked), panel_requests_any_order (the same compared as a multiset, for timers firing in the same tick), panel_request_counts ({request line: how many times}, for long stretches of time), process_exit (exit codes passed to process.exit, in order), logged_error (true: at least one error-level log entry was written during the step, false: none; log text is otherwise not contractual, but a failure described as 'only logged' must be logged), rest_response ({status, body?}; body is the parsed JSON, or the text for non-JSON), and, as absolute states at the end of the step, broker_connects (number of CONNECT packets the bridge has sent so far: the broker has answered each, accepted or refused; with silent, counted where the connection ends), broker_connacks (return codes of the CONNACKs the broker has sent so far, in order: 0 accepted, 5 not authorised), broker_subscriptions (topics subscribed so far, in order), broker_retained ({topic: payload, or null for none}: what a client subscribing now gets from the broker's retained store) and rest_listening (whether the REST port accepts connections). Besides, at the end of every scenario each topic the bridge published with retain holds the last such payload as the broker's retained message. A bug pinned on purpose carries known_bug (catalog id in tests/README.md); those scenarios are in known_bugs/system_scenarios.json, in the same groups. All message payloads are hand-written, nothing is computed by the code under test.",
